WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.119
Why do examples anchor model outputs so much more reliably than instructions?

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.359
That is the question this lesson answers. For the next few minutes,

3
00:00:09.359 --> 00:00:15.520
we'll cover what examples communicate simultaneously, how pattern matching works,

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.360
how format anchoring works in practice, and when to choose zero-shot versus few-shot.

5
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.239
These ideas frame everything we'll do in this lesson.

6
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.040
Show the model one good example of what you want, and you often get better output than a paragraph describing it.

7
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.639
That gap is what few-shot prompting exploits.

8
00:00:34.639 --> 00:00:40.400
Few-shot prompting provides more than one input-output example before the task itself.

9
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.159
The examples demonstrate format, tone, or reasoning,

10
00:00:44.159 --> 00:00:49.279
and high-quality examples are the single biggest lever for output consistency.

11
00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:53.040
Zero-shot prompting issues a task with no examples at all.

12
00:00:53.439 --> 00:00:59.040
It relies on the model's pre-trained knowledge and how clearly the instruction is written,

13
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.080
so it works well for common tasks, but often degrades on novel formats

14
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.160
or domain-specific output structures.

15
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.080
One-shot prompting provides exactly one example.

16
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.959
It's a deliberate choice, not a fallback,

17
00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:17.839
and when a single canonical case fully defines the pattern, one example is all you need.

18
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.919
A well-designed example carries information that prose instructions struggle to convey.

19
00:01:23.919 --> 00:01:28.400
It shows format directly, the exact structure the output should follow,

20
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.279
without requiring the model to interpret a written description of that structure.

21
00:01:33.279 --> 00:01:37.199
It communicates tone through demonstration rather than instruction,

22
00:01:37.199 --> 00:01:40.879
whether that is formal, conversational, terse, or detailed.

23
00:01:40.879 --> 00:01:45.040
It shows the reasoning pattern by example, not explanation,

24
00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:49.360
and it implicitly scopes the output by showing what's included

25
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.760
and what's left out of a correct response.

26
00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:56.000
Prose instructions typically address a single dimension at a time

27
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.239
and leave the others open to interpretation.

28
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.919
An example communicates all four simultaneously,

29
00:02:01.919 --> 00:02:07.040
which is why a single high-quality example often outperforms a full paragraph

30
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.279
of written guidance on format and style.

31
00:02:10.559 --> 00:02:14.960
Pattern matching is a useful way to think about how examples anchor output.

32
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.279
When you provide examples, the model doesn't just read them for surface content.

33
00:02:19.279 --> 00:02:23.839
It picks up on structural patterns, like the length of responses,

34
00:02:23.839 --> 00:02:27.199
the phrasing style, and the sequencing of output elements.

35
00:02:27.919 --> 00:02:32.960
Those implicit patterns carry over even when the new input looks different from the examples.

36
00:02:33.679 --> 00:02:38.880
With a prose instruction, the model has to work out what your description means for this output,

37
00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.039
and that leaves more room for variance.

38
00:02:41.039 --> 00:02:45.440
Particularly for novel or domain-specific formats, the model hasn't seen often.

39
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.559
With an example, the model can mirror a concrete demonstration directly.

40
00:02:50.559 --> 00:02:54.240
That tends to transfer format and tone more reliably,

41
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.800
which is why consistent output is usually easier to achieve with examples

42
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.240
than with instructions alone.

43
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.119
Format anchoring is the name for what a worked example does to the shape of your output.

44
00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:10.559
A prose description of the format has to be interpreted,

45
00:03:10.559 --> 00:03:13.679
and interpretation is where the variance creeps in.

46
00:03:13.679 --> 00:03:15.279
A demonstration doesn't.

47
00:03:15.279 --> 00:03:17.199
The model can mirror it directly.

48
00:03:17.759 --> 00:03:21.839
That difference matters most on structured output tasks,

49
00:03:21.839 --> 00:03:24.720
where you need the response to carry a specific element,

50
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.679
a JSON object, a table row, a labeled field.

51
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.399
Describing that structure in words leaves room for the model

52
00:03:32.399 --> 00:03:35.039
to render it a dozen defensible ways,

53
00:03:35.039 --> 00:03:38.800
and any of them might be reasonable while still not being what you wanted.

54
00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.399
Showing it once removes the ambiguity.

55
00:03:42.399 --> 00:03:46.559
It's also why examples tend to beat instructions for any format

56
00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.080
the model hasn't seen often,

57
00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.440
since there's no common convention for it to fall back on.

58
00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.520
Your example becomes the convention.

59
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.759
Shot count matters, but the returns aren't linear.

60
00:03:57.759 --> 00:04:00.479
Zero shot is fastest and simplest.

61
00:04:00.479 --> 00:04:04.000
When a task is common and format flexibility is acceptable,

62
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.679
you don't need examples at all.

63
00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.759
One example works well when a single canonical case

64
00:04:09.759 --> 00:04:11.119
fully defines the pattern.

65
00:04:11.679 --> 00:04:14.960
Three examples is the most broadly useful configuration

66
00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.959
for structured output tasks

67
00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.320
because it captures enough format and tone variation

68
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.920
without overwhelming the prompt.

69
00:04:22.559 --> 00:04:25.519
Beyond three, returns diminish in most cases.

70
00:04:26.079 --> 00:04:28.799
Five or more examples are worth the overhead

71
00:04:28.799 --> 00:04:32.480
only when your inputs span genuinely different types

72
00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.359
or edge cases that three examples can't cover.

73
00:04:36.079 --> 00:04:38.079
More examples isn't always better.

74
00:04:38.079 --> 00:04:42.000
The right count is the minimum that reliably anchors the pattern you need.

75
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.399
Zero shot and few shot aren't in competition.

76
00:04:46.399 --> 00:04:48.480
They serve different situations.

77
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.359
Zero shot works well for summarization,

78
00:04:51.359 --> 00:04:54.799
question answering, and common text generation tasks

79
00:04:54.799 --> 00:04:56.640
where the format is flexible

80
00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.399
and the task is well within the model's pre-trained knowledge.

81
00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:04.000
Few shot is the right choice when you need structured output,

82
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.320
novel formats, or consistent tone across a range of inputs.

83
00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.399
For well-defined common tasks,

84
00:05:10.399 --> 00:05:14.399
zero shot with clear instructions can match few shot performance

85
00:05:14.399 --> 00:05:16.399
and it's simpler to maintain.

86
00:05:16.399 --> 00:05:19.359
The practical approach is to start with zero shot

87
00:05:19.359 --> 00:05:22.559
and add examples only when you observe output variance

88
00:05:22.559 --> 00:05:25.279
you can't fix with instruction improvements.

89
00:05:25.279 --> 00:05:27.279
Few shot has real overhead,

90
00:05:27.279 --> 00:05:30.640
so use it when the consistency payoff justifies it.

91
00:05:30.959 --> 00:05:34.320
The relationship between example count and output quality

92
00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.920
isn't what most people assume.

93
00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.279
One strong example that covers the target format,

94
00:05:39.279 --> 00:05:42.079
edge case behavior, and desired tone

95
00:05:42.079 --> 00:05:44.079
will outperform five weak ones.

96
00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.279
When your examples have inconsistent formatting,

97
00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:50.799
off-tone phrasing, or vary in what they include,

98
00:05:50.799 --> 00:05:53.200
you're giving the model conflicting signals.

99
00:05:53.679 --> 00:05:56.000
The model averages across those signals

100
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.839
and the result is output that's muddled rather than consistent.

101
00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.239
Before adding more examples to a failing prompt,

102
00:06:04.239 --> 00:06:06.640
audit the examples you already have.

103
00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:09.200
The question isn't how many examples you need,

104
00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.399
it's whether the examples you have are doing their job.

105
00:06:12.399 --> 00:06:15.279
Quality is the lever that moves output the most.

106
00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.679
There's a real gap between describing the output you want and showing it.

107
00:06:21.679 --> 00:06:25.440
A description leaves the model an interpretation step

108
00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.239
and interpretation is where variance creeps in.

109
00:06:28.799 --> 00:06:31.200
A demonstration skips that entirely,

110
00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.000
which is why structure holds instead of drifting.

111
00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:37.600
That difference is also why one clean example

112
00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:40.959
beats a handful of sloppy ones, so be specific.

113
00:06:41.679 --> 00:06:43.040
That does it for this lesson.

114
00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.559
I'll see you in the next one.

